WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.274
Every request to the Kubernetes API passes three gates, in order.

2
00:00:07.274 --> 00:00:11.567
Only then is anything stored in etcd, the cluster's database.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.895
Gate one is authentication.

4
00:00:13.895 --> 00:00:14.827
Who are you?

5
00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:17.388
A certificate or a token answers.

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.838
Gate two is authorization.

7
00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:21.216
May you do this?

8
00:00:21.216 --> 00:00:22.335
RBAC answers.

9
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.217
Gate three is admission.

10
00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:26.761
Is this particular object acceptable?

11
00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.603
Start with RBAC, role-based access control.

12
00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:33.539
For every request, it answers one question.

13
00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:36.817
May this identity do this verb on this resource?

14
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.489
It uses four objects.

15
00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:43.896
A Role lists rules, such as get, list and watch on pods, inside one namespace.

16
00:00:43.896 --> 00:00:47.293
A ClusterRole is the same, for the whole cluster.

17
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.647
A RoleBinding gives a Role, or a ClusterRole, to a subject in one namespace.

18
00:00:52.647 --> 00:00:57.049
A ClusterRoleBinding gives a ClusterRole to a subject everywhere.

19
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:01.354
The subject is a user, a group, or a ServiceAccount.

20
00:01:01.354 --> 00:01:07.328
A ServiceAccount is an identity for a program: a Pod, a CI job, an AI agent.

21
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:10.420
Here is a real one, from the lab.

22
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.601
A ServiceAccount called dev, in namespace hello.

23
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:15.506
A Role called pod-creator.

24
00:01:15.506 --> 00:01:19.946
It may get, list, watch, create and delete Pods, and read their logs.

25
00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.555
No rule mentions Secrets.

26
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:25.848
And a RoleBinding, dev-pod-creator, that gives the Role to dev.

27
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:29.295
Before you rely on a rule, test it.

28
00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:32.763
kubectl auth can-i asks the API server directly.

29
00:01:32.763 --> 00:01:35.725
The as flag acts as dev, for one request.

30
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:38.064
May dev get secrets?

31
00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:38.383
No.

32
00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:40.513
May dev create pods?

33
00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:40.939
Yes.

34
00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:42.431
It looks safe.

35
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:45.593
Now dev creates a Pod called peek.

36
00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:50.643
It loads the db-password Secret into an environment variable, and prints it.

37
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:52.710
RBAC checks one thing.

38
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.385
May dev create Pods?

39
00:01:54.385 --> 00:01:54.721
Yes.

40
00:01:54.721 --> 00:01:57.485
So the Pod passes, and is stored.

41
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.637
Then the kubelet fetches the Secret for the Pod.

42
00:02:00.637 --> 00:02:02.961
Nobody checks dev's rights to Secrets.

43
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:08.059
dev reads the Pod's log, and there is the password the Role was meant to protect.

44
00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.722
So the real boundary is the namespace.

45
00:02:10.722 --> 00:02:14.211
Anyone who may create Pods there can read its Secrets.

46
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:17.482
That is why least privilege matters.

47
00:02:17.482 --> 00:02:19.961
Give each identity only what it needs.

48
00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:22.961
The official good practices fit in four lines.

49
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:27.385
Bind inside a namespace, with a RoleBinding, whenever you can.

50
00:02:27.385 --> 00:02:28.474
Avoid wildcards.

51
00:02:28.474 --> 00:02:33.917
A star grants every resource type that exists today, and every type added later.

52
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:36.691
Do not use cluster-admin for daily work.

53
00:02:36.691 --> 00:02:44.237
And know the rights that are bigger than they look: creating Pods, nodes proxy, and the verbs that change RBAC itself.

54
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:50.919
Say a CI job only needs to update the image of Deployments in namespace shop.

55
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:52.306
Not cluster-admin.

56
00:02:52.306 --> 00:02:56.736
A Role in shop, with get and patch on deployments, and nothing more.

57
00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.595
Two habits for ServiceAccounts.

58
00:02:59.595 --> 00:03:03.063
Give each app its own, never the namespace's default.

59
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:09.411
And if the app never calls the Kubernetes API, set automountServiceAccountToken to false.

60
00:03:09.411 --> 00:03:12.517
Then a broken-into Pod has no token to steal.

61
00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:14.712
Now the third gate.

62
00:03:14.712 --> 00:03:17.942
RBAC decides whether you may create Pods at all.

63
00:03:17.942 --> 00:03:21.240
Admission decides whether this Pod is acceptable.

64
00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:29.601
Admission controllers can change an object, called mutating, or reject it, called validating, before it is stored in etcd.

65
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:34.188
Kyverno runs your own rules at this gate, as a webhook.

66
00:03:34.188 --> 00:03:40.196
The API server sends it each matching request, and waits for the answer: allow, or deny.

67
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.657
The lab's policy, disallow-latest-tag, denies any image tagged latest, in namespace hello.

68
00:03:46.867 --> 00:03:48.175
Watch one request.

69
00:03:48.175 --> 00:03:52.320
Someone runs a test Pod with the image nginx, tag latest.

70
00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:54.111
Authentication passes.

71
00:03:54.111 --> 00:03:56.978
RBAC says this identity may create Pods.

72
00:03:56.978 --> 00:04:00.276
Then Kyverno checks the object, and says deny.

73
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.126
The request is rejected, with the policy's message.

74
00:04:04.126 --> 00:04:05.975
Nothing is stored in etcd.

75
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.560
Try again with the image busybox, tag 1.37.

76
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.232
All three gates say yes, and the Pod is stored.

77
00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:19.957
Kubernetes also has a built-in admission check for Pod hardening, Pod Security Admission.

78
00:04:19.957 --> 00:04:23.817
It has three levels: privileged, baseline, and restricted.

79
00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:26.888
You switch it on per namespace, with a label.

80
00:04:26.888 --> 00:04:32.089
Enforce rejects, warn shows the user a warning, and audit writes to the audit log.

81
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.808
Roll out every new rule the same way.

82
00:04:34.808 --> 00:04:38.671
Audit first, read the reports, fix what fails, then Deny.

83
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.567
Authentication asks who you are.

84
00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:43.329
RBAC asks what you may do.

85
00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:46.647
Admission asks whether this object is acceptable.

86
00:04:46.647 --> 00:04:49.560
And every identity gets only what it needs.
